Package: dflimit
Type: Package
Title: Exact Limiting Distribution of the Dickey-Fuller t-Ratio
Version: 0.5.0
Authors@R: c(
    person("Yong", "Bao", role = c("aut", "cph")),
    person("Simon A.", "Broda", email = "simon.broda@hslu.ch", role = c("aut", "cre", "cph")),
    person("Raymond", "Kan", role = c("aut", "cph")),
    person("Steven G.", "Johnson", role = "ctb",
           comment = "author of the Faddeeva package in src/Faddeeva.cc and src/Faddeeva.h"),
    person("Massachusetts Institute of Technology", role = "cph",
           comment = "copyright holder of the Faddeeva package"))
Description: The distribution function, density, quantiles and moments of the
    limits of the Dickey-Fuller t-ratios under local alternatives are computed
    for the regression without deterministic terms, with an intercept, and with
    an intercept and a trend, with a zero, a fixed or a stationary initial
    observation, and for the t-ratio centred at a local null value. Also
    covered are the trend case of Elliott, Rothenberg and Stock (1996)
    <doi:10.2307/2171846>, with the data detrended by generalized least squares
    (GLS); the ADFmax statistic of Leybourne (1995)
    <doi:10.1111/j.1468-0084.1995.tb00040.x>, the larger of the t-ratios of the
    forward and the reverse regression; and the probabilities of regions of the
    limit experiment and its asymptotic power envelope. A second engine
    computes the same limits for any piecewise smooth deterministic terms
    (polynomial trends of any order, breaks in level and slope at known
    dates, GLS detrending).
    The values come from the exact representations of Bao, Broda and Kan
    (2026) <https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=7745921>,
    "The Exact Limiting Distribution of the Dickey-Fuller t-Ratio with General
    Deterministic Terms", and, for the classical cases, of their companion
    paper (2026) <https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=7725619>,
    "Low-Rank Representations of the Classical Dickey-Fuller Limits, with an
    Extension to Predictive Regressions", evaluated by a multithreaded engine
    written in 'C'.
